22	ASSISTANT	The fundamental difference between a Python **list** and a **tuple** comes down to one main concept: **mutability** (whether or not the data can be changed after it is created).\n\n---\n\n### The Main Differences\n\n| Feature | List | Tuple |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Mutability** | **Mutable** (Can be changed, added to, or removed from) | **Immutable** (Cannot be changed once created) |\n| **Syntax** | Uses square brackets: `[1, 2, 3]` | Uses parentheses: `(1, 2, 3)` |\n| **Performance** | Slightly slower, uses more memory | Slightly faster, uses less memory |\n| **Built-in Methods**| Has many methods (`append()`, `remove()`, `sort()`, etc.) | Has very few methods (`count()`, `index()`) |\n\n---\n\n### When to use a LIST (with Example)\n\nUse a **list** when you have a collection of items that **will change** over time—meaning you need to add, remove, or modify elements.\n\n**Example: A Shopping Cart**\nAs a user browses an online store, they add and remove items from their cart. \n\n```python\n# Create a list of items in a shopping cart\ncart = ["apples", "milk", "bread"]\n\n# Add an item to the list\ncart.append("eggs")\n\n# Remove an item from the list\ncart.remove("milk")\n\nprint(cart)  # Output: ['apples', 'bread', 'eggs']\n```\n\n---\n\n### When to use a TUPLE (with Example)\n\nUse a **tuple** when you have a collection of items that **should never change**. Tuples act as a safety net to prevent accidental modifications to fixed data (like coordinates, date of birth, or database records).\n\n**Example: GPS Coordinates**\nA specific location on Earth is defined by a pair of numbers (latitude and longitude) that should always stay together and never change.\n\n```python\n# Create a tuple for the location of the Eiffel Tower (Latitude, Longitude)\neiffel_tower_location = (48.8584, 2.2945)\n\n# If you try to change one of the values, Python throws an error:\n# eiffel_tower_location[0] = 50.0  \n# TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\n---\n\n### Rule of Thumb\n* If your data is a **dynamic collection** that will grow, shrink, or be reordered $\\rightarrow$ **Use a List**.\n* If your data is a **fixed structure** or constant values that belong together $\\rightarrow$ **Use a Tuple**.	2026-08-11 10:54:48.969373+00	8
